# Ant 005: world-Y velocity steering, with failed formal acceptance

This is the current generated account of the existing result. It supersedes the interpretation in the [historical narrative](https://github.com/BurnyCoder/reinforcement-learning-locomotion-steering-vectors/blob/fc8cf63/reports/ant-classic-005-findings.md); original numerical artifacts and protocol decisions are unchanged. Author: Libor “Burny” Burian.

## Question and method

The question was whether a frozen Ant SAC actor contains an activation contrast that produces a useful, repeatable change in lateral movement or turning. “Lateral” is the original vector key; its evaluation coordinate is **world-Y ground-plane velocity**, not a body-relative sidestep. The [locally dated protocol](ant-classic-005-preregistration.md) records the pilot hypotheses and quality gates. Its first Git record follows the start of diagnostics/fitting, so it is not independently verifiable preregistration before any results.

The checkpoint has {{observation_dim}} observations, {{action_dim}} actions, and a first hidden layer of {{hidden_dim}} ReLU units. It is the [pinned Farama-Minari SAC checkpoint](https://huggingface.co/farama-minari/Ant-v5-SAC-medium/tree/{{checkpoint_revision}}), SHA256 `{{checkpoint_sha}}`. We use deterministic inference and do not update policy weights. Upstream training provenance is distinct from independently reproduced training history.

After {{diagnostic_episodes}} diagnostic and {{fit_episodes}} fitting episodes, the extraction uses complete {{window}}-step windows following a {{warmup}}-step warmup. {{eligible_windows}}/{{total_windows}} fitting windows are eligible under the saved rules. The high/low quartile activation means are episode-weighted, differenced, and scaled to the fitting activation RMS. The saved lateral norm is {{norm}}; strength {{alpha}} applies an offset of norm {{offset_norm}}. Its split-half cosine is {{split_half}}. These stability diagnostics describe extraction, not causal usefulness.

The implementation adapts [contrastive activation addition](https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.06681) to continuous-action locomotion. Earlier [maze-policy work](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08043) and [top-right steering](https://turntrout.com/top-right-steering-vector) already intervened on frozen RL activations. Episode weighting, movement filters, RMS scaling and usefulness gates are project choices, documented in [methodology](../docs/methodology.md) and [sources](../docs/sources.md).

## Data separation and selection

{{cohort_table}}

The original run evaluated {{n_validation}} validation conditions and selected the lateral strength {{alpha}} before confirmation. {{n_confirmation}} conditions were evaluated in confirmation; each comparator retained its own validation-selected strength. The fitting data determine vectors, validation determines strength, and confirmation estimates the locked outcomes. The later audit has separate provenance and must not be pooled with confirmation or described as the unused formal replication split.

## Original confirmation results

The primary shifts mean world-Y velocity from {{baseline_y}} to {{treated_y}} m/s: **{{effect}} m/s**, paired percentile-bootstrap 95% interval **{{ci}}**. Mean world-X speed changes from {{baseline_speed}} to {{treated_speed}} m/s, retaining **{{retention}}%**. This is speed retention, not distance retention or a gait-similarity measurement.

{{original_table}}

The baseline is shared across conditions. The shuffled control is not a label-free null: its construction retains the fitting activation geometry. Random controls and the action-space comparator show that generic perturbations can also move the path. Failure costs and differing selected strengths prevent reading the largest effect as the best controller.

The status is **`{{status}}`**. Every pair is retained. An episode ending before intervention receives zero for unavailable post-onset summary outcomes and still counts as a failure; the strict prefix criterion independently rejects the condition. Other episodes use their available post-onset steps. This convention is not a general missing-data correction or a fixed-horizon estimand. Baseline and treated failure counts are each {{baseline_failures}}/{{n}}, with different failed-seed identities. Squared-action effort rises {{effort_change}}%; mean post-onset reward falls from {{baseline_reward}} to {{treated_reward}} per step. No practical-target application is claimed.

